  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\n     We address the Erd\u0151s minimum overlap problem by employing a multimodal optimization strategy combined with adaptive coordinate scaling. The key idea is to:\n\n     a) Use a random search on increasing grid resolutions, starting from coarse to finer grids while maintaining the parent's symmetry\n     b) Apply directed coordinate descent only in regions of high gradient\n     c) Use adaptive scaling of each coordinate based on its correlation impact\n     d) Combine multi-start with local optimization and grid refinement\n\n     This approach directly tackles the non-convex nature of the problem and avoids local optima common in gradient-based methods. The central hypothesis is that adaptive coordinate handling and multi-resolution shifting will yield a tighter bound than random restarts or coordinate-wise descent alone.\n\n  2. Mathematical Specification\n     The optimization problem:\n\n       minimize_C5(h) = max_{k} \u222b[0,2] h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx\n\n     subject to:\n         h: [0,2] \u2192 [0,1]\n         \u222b[0,2] h(x) dx = 1\n\n     In discrete form with n grid points:\n\n        minimize max_k ( (np.correlate(h,1-h,mode='full')) * (2.0/n) )\n        with 0 <= h[i] <= 1, \u2211h[i]==n/2\n\n     We represent h as a real-valued function, relaxing the step constraint, then impose it through projection.\n\n     We use a two-phase approach:\n        Phase 1: RandSweep(coord_scale_factor, grid_points)\n        Phase 2: DirCD(threshold, penalty, grid_points)\n\n     The objective is minimized via:\n\n        PenaltyObjective(h) = max_k correlation + \u03bb\u00b7(smooth_gradient(h))\n\n     with \u03bb as an adaptive penalty parameter.\n\n  3. Implementation Procedure\n     a. Initialization\n        If parent provided: use initial_h_values (n=600)\n        Else: initialize with uniform distribution then project to satisfy constraints\n\n     b. Core Algorithm Loop\n        Let n = current grid size (starts at 600)\n        Let best_h = initial feasible solution\n        Let best_c = compute_c5(best_h, n)\n\n        Stage 1: Random exploration\n          For iteration in range(max_iter):\n            if time > deadline: return best_solution\n            if random < 0.2: apply_symmetry(best_h)\n\n            # Main improvement:RandSweep\n            new_h = random_step(best_h, n) \n            threshold = 0.95 if grad_F > 0.8 else 0.1\n\n            if abs(compute_c5(new_h, n) - best_c) < 1e-5:\n                continue\n\n            if several_satisfied(new_h, n):\n                new_h = project_feasible(new_h, n)\n                if compute_c5(new_h, n) < best_c:\n                    best_h, best_c = new_h, compute_c5(new_h, n)\n                    save_solution(best_h, best_c)\n\n        Stage 2: Directed coordinate search\n          For coord in range(n):\n            if not at_minimum(best_c):\n                scale = calculate_optimal_scale(best_h, coord, penalty_factor)\n                apply_adaptive_change(best_h, coord, scale)\n                best_h[coord] = np.clip(best_h[coord], 0, 1)\n                new_c = compute_c5(best_h, n)\n                if new_c < best_c:\n                    best_c = new_c\n                    save_solution(best_h, best_c)\n\n        Stage 3: Grid refinement\n          if (n <= 1200) and (time_left > 300):\n              n = n * 2\n              dx = 2.0/n\n              best_h = resample(best_h, n)\n              project_feasible(best_h, n)\n\n     c. Restart protocol\n        Every 60 seconds:\n          if no improvement: start_new_point(best_h)\n\n        For grid refinement:\n          if grid_refinement_needed(best_c): increase_grid()\n\n  4. Search Choices and Variations\n     a) Core directions:\n        - symmetrical transformations: always try 1-h after major changes\n        - multi-start frequency: every 600 seconds for grid>=200\n        - grid increase trigger: when best_c stagnates or grid<1000\n\n     b) Key parameterizations:\n        * coord_scale_factor \u2208 [0.01, 0.3]\n        * penalty_factor \u2208 [5, 30]\n        * depth_multiplier \u2208 [1.5, 3] for aggressive vs conservative\n\n     c) Dimensions:\n        - We must handle arrays of varying lengths [200, 1600]\n        - Each refinement increases grid by doubling\n\n     d) Monitoring conditions:\n        - Stop after 5 hours or explicit timeout\n        - Check for improvement every 2 seconds\n        - If any coordinate has absolute change > 0.5, scale penalty\n\n  5. Compute Budget and Robustness\n        Total budget: 1100 seconds\n        Basic configuration:\n          Start with n=600 [given parent]\n          Iterate until 2400 seconds total\n          Allow grid increases to 1200 points\n\n        Time distribution:\n          - Exploration: 60% time\n          - Directed search: 30% time\n          - Grid refinement: 10% time\n\n        Deadline checks:\n          - Before each sample/descent\n          - After each stochastic step\n          - In grid refinement assessment\n\n        Recovery:\n          - If infeasibility detected, restart from best_parent\n          - If timeout before valid solution, return greediest feasible\n\n  6. Validation and Return Contract\n        a. Feasibility checks:\n            - 0\u2264h[i]\u22641: validate_array_bounds\n            - Integral match: |sum(h)-n/2|<1e-6\n            - Correlation calculation: use provided np.correlate mode='full'\n\n        b. Directed operation monitoring:\n            - compute_c5 called at least yearly (every 50 seconds)\n            - Objective validation: np.isfinite(c5) and c5<0.45\n\n        c. Early termination conditions:\n            - Improvement plateau (300 seconds without improvement)\n            - Time exceeded\n            - Feasibility loss\n\n        d. Solution representation:\n            - np.array of real numbers\n            - length n (consistent across all evaluations)\n            - grid spacing maintained\n\n     Note: All transformations are dimension-sensitive and must preserve [0,2] range with equal spacing.\n\n     The algorithm intelligently coordinates between coarse grid versatility and fine grid precision by dynamically adjusting solution parameters based on local gradient and global score. This differs from simpler methods by explicitly handling dimension-normalized correlation terms and grid-aware evaluation with adaptive coordinate-wise division of labor.",
